WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.567 --> 00:00:02.567
-It is a pleasure to meet you.
I'm a big fan of yours.

00:00:02.567 --> 00:00:05.000
I got to tell you. "The Menu."

00:00:05.000 --> 00:00:06.601
You were so fantastic
in that movie.

00:00:06.601 --> 00:00:08.300
-Hey. Thank you very much.
-I loved it.

00:00:08.300 --> 00:00:10.133
If you haven't seen that movie,
check this out.

00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:12.300
-Yeah, it's good.

00:00:12.300 --> 00:00:15.567
-I didn't know what it was
gonna be about going into it.

00:00:15.567 --> 00:00:17.100
I just thought, like,
it was people eating

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:18.734
at a fancy restaurant
or something.

00:00:18.734 --> 00:00:20.100
-Well, it is kind of about that.

00:00:20.100 --> 00:00:22.367
Like, the worst guests
possible at a fancy restaurant

00:00:22.367 --> 00:00:23.868
and everything
that's wrong with them.

00:00:23.868 --> 00:00:25.467
And I was one of
the terrible guests.

00:00:25.467 --> 00:00:26.934
-But, gosh, you made me laugh.

00:00:26.934 --> 00:00:29.000
It's very funny, by the way,
I should mention, too.

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:30.901
-It is funny.
-A very twisted dark comedy.

00:00:30.901 --> 00:00:33.167
-Yeah, but, gosh, you made me
laugh when you started --

00:00:33.167 --> 00:00:34.634
It reminded me
a little bit of me

00:00:34.634 --> 00:00:36.467
because I watched it
with my wife,

00:00:36.467 --> 00:00:38.367
and I loved those shows
like "Chef's Table,"

00:00:38.367 --> 00:00:40.133
all that stuff,
and they're like,

00:00:40.133 --> 00:00:43.000
"This, you know,
scallop was found."

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:45.767
And I'm like,
"Oh, it's just beautiful."

00:00:45.767 --> 00:00:47.734
And your char--
And my wife's like,

00:00:47.734 --> 00:00:49.367
"It's a scallop,"
or, you know, whatever.

00:00:49.367 --> 00:00:51.100
But I always freak out.
I got, "Oh, my God."

00:00:51.100 --> 00:00:52.334
You know,
I love those shows.

00:00:52.334 --> 00:00:54.767
And your character
at one point starts crying

00:00:54.767 --> 00:00:56.400
when he's --
-He does.

00:00:56.400 --> 00:00:58.267
He gets -- He gets misty-eyed.
He gets emotional about it.

00:00:58.267 --> 00:01:00.234
-He gets misty-eyed, emotional.
It's so funny.

00:01:00.234 --> 00:01:01.834
-Yeah.
-And then it gets --

00:01:01.834 --> 00:01:04.133
It gets nuts. But, man,
you were fantastic in that.

00:01:04.133 --> 00:01:06.267
-Thank you very much.
That's very kind of you.

00:01:06.267 --> 00:01:09.601
-I have a picture of one scene
from the movie.

00:01:09.601 --> 00:01:12.033
-This -- So, this --
This is one of my -- This --

00:01:12.033 --> 00:01:14.100
-So it is funny. I'm telling
you, it is a comedy, but...

00:01:14.100 --> 00:01:15.834
-This is one
of my proudest moments

00:01:15.834 --> 00:01:17.667
because this image
has kind of become a meme,

00:01:17.667 --> 00:01:20.467
and I never expected
to become a meme for this.

00:01:20.467 --> 00:01:21.934
-Congratulations.
-But, also,

00:01:21.934 --> 00:01:23.601
it's kind of ridiculous
because this is a moment

00:01:23.601 --> 00:01:25.868
when I've been forced to cook
in the film

00:01:25.868 --> 00:01:28.100
and my character,
who thinks he knows

00:01:28.100 --> 00:01:29.934
everything about cooking,
has been forced to cook.

00:01:29.934 --> 00:01:32.200
And he's terrible at it.
Truly terrible.

00:01:32.200 --> 00:01:35.801
So this has become a meme for
people who are bad at things.

00:01:35.801 --> 00:01:37.434
[ Laughter ]
Which I never --

00:01:37.434 --> 00:01:40.267
-No, no. It doesn't mean you.
No, no. It's the character.

00:01:40.267 --> 00:01:42.067
-I'm the face of, like, someone
who's bad at something.

00:01:42.067 --> 00:01:43.300
-No! Not at all. Not at all.

00:01:43.300 --> 00:01:44.534
-And I was like,
"Damn. How did this happen?"

00:01:44.534 --> 00:01:45.834
But also I'm very proud of it.

00:01:45.834 --> 00:01:47.300
-He's whispering something
in your ear.

00:01:47.300 --> 00:01:50.133
Do we -- We never hear what
he's whispering in your ear.

00:01:50.133 --> 00:01:51.501
Is that --
-No, no, no, it wasn't --

00:01:51.501 --> 00:01:53.868
It wasn't scripted,
what he whispers in my ear.

00:01:53.868 --> 00:01:55.601
And Ralph is fantastic.

00:01:55.601 --> 00:01:57.133
-Ralph Fiennes.
He's one of the greatest.

00:01:57.133 --> 00:01:59.000
He was phenomenal in this,
as well.

00:01:59.000 --> 00:02:00.968
-Yeah.
And he -- and he would --

00:02:00.968 --> 00:02:03.601
he would deliver
different things...

00:02:03.601 --> 00:02:05.567
-To get different emotions?
-...different things in my ear.

00:02:05.567 --> 00:02:07.834
-I wonder what whispers
to get this scared --

00:02:07.834 --> 00:02:10.901
Look at that.
You're a great actor, bud.

00:02:10.901 --> 00:02:12.801
-You don't want to know
what he was saying.

00:02:12.801 --> 00:02:14.567
-I don't want to know.
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

00:02:14.567 --> 00:02:16.267
I want to know about this,
by the way.

00:02:16.267 --> 00:02:18.000
I follow you on the socials
on Twitter.

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:20.667
And I looked at your --
your Twitter bio, okay?

00:02:20.667 --> 00:02:22.868
-By the way,
it's like 15 years old.

00:02:22.868 --> 00:02:25.133
-Yeah.
-I should probably update that.

00:02:25.133 --> 00:02:26.734
-Yeah, exactly.

00:02:26.734 --> 00:02:27.968
But this one here says

00:02:27.968 --> 00:02:30.033
"Marble egg enthusiast,"
which --

00:02:30.033 --> 00:02:31.167
-Yeah.

00:02:31.167 --> 00:02:32.334
-Now, is that assuming,
like, marbles?

00:02:32.334 --> 00:02:33.901
Is that what that is?
-You know, just like

00:02:33.901 --> 00:02:35.534
marble, the stone.
-Oh.

00:02:35.534 --> 00:02:37.934
-But just eggs.
[ Laughter ]

00:02:37.934 --> 00:02:39.300
They're not interesting.
I think when I was a kid

00:02:39.300 --> 00:02:40.501
I thought they would, like,

00:02:40.501 --> 00:02:41.834
maybe hatch dragons
or something.

00:02:41.834 --> 00:02:43.734
So I liked them,
and I had a little collection.

00:02:43.734 --> 00:02:45.701
Aww. Thank you. Yeah.

00:02:45.701 --> 00:02:47.434
-So you really mean
marble eggs?

00:02:47.434 --> 00:02:49.501
-Just -- They don't do anything,
and I'd just get them out

00:02:49.501 --> 00:02:51.501
as a kid and hold them,
and then I'd put them back away

00:02:51.501 --> 00:02:53.467
and keep them safe,
and it's like -- I don't know.

00:02:53.467 --> 00:02:55.267
-Maybe they were dragon eggs.
We don't know.

00:02:55.267 --> 00:02:56.934
I mean, they could be
hatching right now.

00:02:56.934 --> 00:02:58.334
-I'm like Gollum
with my, like, little --

00:02:58.334 --> 00:03:00.067
-Yes. Exactly.
"Game of Thrones."

00:03:00.067 --> 00:03:02.300
Whoa. Wow. I didn't know
you meant marble eggs.

00:03:02.300 --> 00:03:03.634
-I shouldn't have put that
in my bio now I think about it.

00:03:03.634 --> 00:03:04.934
-No, I like that a lot.

00:03:04.934 --> 00:03:07.200
Next time, we should have
marble eggs on and look at them.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.601
-Please. Yeah.
-But then the first thing

00:03:08.601 --> 00:03:10.033
really made me laugh.

00:03:10.033 --> 00:03:14.234
"Berkshire County Athletics
2005 discus bronze medalist."

00:03:14.234 --> 00:03:15.300
-Yeah.

00:03:15.300 --> 00:03:16.467
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:03:16.467 --> 00:03:18.167
-So...

00:03:18.167 --> 00:03:20.334
-You're in the presence
of greatness.

00:03:20.334 --> 00:03:22.133
[ Laughter ]

00:03:22.133 --> 00:03:23.601
-There he is.
Winning the --

00:03:23.601 --> 00:03:25.434
-Good stitch-back.
That was nice. Well played.

00:03:25.434 --> 00:03:27.701
-How did you win
the bronze medal there?

00:03:27.701 --> 00:03:30.167
-I know. It's surprising
I didn't get the gold.

00:03:30.167 --> 00:03:32.200
The weird thing was,
it was only the second time

00:03:32.200 --> 00:03:34.467
I think I'd ever really,
ever thrown a discus, as well.

00:03:34.467 --> 00:03:37.267
I did it once in P.E.,
and then they were like,

00:03:37.267 --> 00:03:38.534
"Oh, you threw it kind of okay.

00:03:38.534 --> 00:03:40.300
Do you want to do ---
Do you want to do County?"

00:03:40.300 --> 00:03:42.400
And I was like, "Yeah, alright."

00:03:42.400 --> 00:03:44.267
And I never even learned
how to do, like,

00:03:44.267 --> 00:03:46.634
the spinning proper throw.
-You didn't even spin it.

00:03:46.634 --> 00:03:48.734
-I just stood there
and went, "Ugh!"

00:03:48.734 --> 00:03:49.868
And that was it.

00:03:49.868 --> 00:03:51.634
But then the people
that were there

00:03:51.634 --> 00:03:53.200
that were actually
great discus throwers

00:03:53.200 --> 00:03:54.934
who obviously --
that was, like, their skill --

00:03:54.934 --> 00:03:56.534
they kept --
You know, if you walk out

00:03:56.534 --> 00:03:58.601
the front of the circle, you
foul out, and it doesn't count.

00:03:58.601 --> 00:03:59.701
-Disqualified.

00:03:59.701 --> 00:04:00.934
-They kept on
getting disqualified.

00:04:00.934 --> 00:04:03.033
So it was kind of by default.

00:04:03.033 --> 00:04:04.667
I ended up
getting the bronze medal.

00:04:04.667 --> 00:04:06.200
-Yeah, that's what I'm talk--
That's a winner!

00:04:06.200 --> 00:04:08.334
-I've still got the medal,
and I have it with pride.

00:04:08.334 --> 00:04:11.067
-See? Never give up.
Never give up.

00:04:11.067 --> 00:04:13.467
That's the --
That's the point there.

00:04:13.467 --> 00:04:15.834
I want to talk about
your new movie, "Renfield."

00:04:15.834 --> 00:04:18.434
This, again, is a comedy.
-Yes.

00:04:18.434 --> 00:04:21.133
-Dark comedy.
-Yeah, it's a -- it's a comedy

00:04:21.133 --> 00:04:23.400
about kind of
this toxic relationship,

00:04:23.400 --> 00:04:25.968
this narcissistic boss, Dracula.

00:04:25.968 --> 00:04:28.067
Kind of the worst boss
you could imagine.

00:04:28.067 --> 00:04:29.634
-Yeah.
-Played by Nicolas Cage.

00:04:29.634 --> 00:04:31.968
-Nicolas Cage as Dracula!
Come on, bud!

00:04:31.968 --> 00:04:33.801
-Yeah. It's amazing.
-He's the best!

00:04:33.801 --> 00:04:36.701
-I don't think my career
will ever get better than, like,

00:04:36.701 --> 00:04:38.534
being in a scene...
-Look at this.

00:04:38.534 --> 00:04:42.067
-...with the iconic Nicolas Cage
playing Dracula.

00:04:42.067 --> 00:04:43.267
It's just like...

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:45.300
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.100
And he's having so much fun
and he's brilliant in the movie.

00:04:48.100 --> 00:04:50.133
So it was amazing to shoot it.

00:04:50.133 --> 00:04:52.534
And it's an action comedy
about my character

00:04:52.534 --> 00:04:54.000
kind of finding
this support group

00:04:54.000 --> 00:04:56.701
and this network to try
and try and believe in himself

00:04:56.701 --> 00:04:59.701
and escape this boss
who has him,

00:04:59.701 --> 00:05:02.467
you know, killing people
to feed his bloodlust.

00:05:02.467 --> 00:05:04.400
-Yeah. Yeah, we could
all relate to that.

00:05:04.400 --> 00:05:05.601
Yes, of course.

00:05:05.601 --> 00:05:07.100
-Your typical story.

00:05:07.100 --> 00:05:08.901
-Your character gets
special powers.

00:05:08.901 --> 00:05:11.801
Not by eating people's blood,
though.

00:05:11.801 --> 00:05:14.033
-No.
-You eat insects, bugs?

00:05:14.033 --> 00:05:15.834
-Yes. Yeah, yeah, yeah.
That's kind of a classic thing

00:05:15.834 --> 00:05:19.567
from the the Bram Stoker novel,
Renfield consumes life.

00:05:19.567 --> 00:05:21.234
And for our story,
it's been updated.

00:05:21.234 --> 00:05:23.767
It's 100 years on and Renfield
kind of gets

00:05:23.767 --> 00:05:26.334
superhuman abilities,
I guess, from eating bugs

00:05:26.334 --> 00:05:29.300
So there was a fair amount
of bug eating.

00:05:29.300 --> 00:05:32.534
-Yeah. It's a silly question
for me to ask

00:05:32.534 --> 00:05:34.200
because I shouldn't
have said this earlier.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:37.267
I said, "Did you eat
real bugs on set?"

00:05:37.267 --> 00:05:39.367
-Yes. Yes.
-And you said you did.

00:05:39.367 --> 00:05:41.934
-I did, yeah. Yeah.
No, so it depends.

00:05:41.934 --> 00:05:43.200
The props department
were incredible.

00:05:43.200 --> 00:05:44.901
And they did build, like,
for cockroaches,

00:05:44.901 --> 00:05:47.801
we had, like, caramel ones
that looked very authentic.

00:05:47.801 --> 00:05:49.801
So they were yummy.
For the worms,

00:05:49.801 --> 00:05:52.200
it was like gummy worms in,
like, crushed up

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.767
Oreos to look like earth.
So again, very yummy.

00:05:55.767 --> 00:05:58.367
The one bad one was potato bugs.

00:05:58.367 --> 00:06:01.634
Potato bugs were -- They were
real and they were disgusting.

00:06:01.634 --> 00:06:04.667
That was not a nice flavor.
But I have brought some along,

00:06:04.667 --> 00:06:08.267
some of the yummier ones
for you to try if you fancy.

00:06:08.267 --> 00:06:11.100
These are crickets. Crickets.
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:11.100 --> 00:06:13.734
Take them out. Take them out.
So these are like --

00:06:13.734 --> 00:06:15.367
These are dried
and they're flavored.

00:06:15.367 --> 00:06:16.868
They're, um...

00:06:16.868 --> 00:06:19.400
Voilà.

00:06:19.400 --> 00:06:21.968
We've got salt and vinegar
and cheese and bacon,

00:06:21.968 --> 00:06:24.300
I think, are the flavors.

00:06:24.300 --> 00:06:27.367
-I really don't want
to do this, but I will.

00:06:27.367 --> 00:06:30.000
-Now, I thought my bug-eating
days were over, but now,

00:06:30.000 --> 00:06:31.400
like, doing the press tour...

00:06:31.400 --> 00:06:32.534
-You've eaten more bugs
than you have in the movie.

00:06:32.534 --> 00:06:34.067
-Everyone's like,
"Eat another bug."

00:06:34.067 --> 00:06:37.067
-[ Laughs ] "Eat another bug."
-So here I am and I'll do it.

00:06:37.067 --> 00:06:38.834
You know what?

00:06:38.834 --> 00:06:45.200
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:47.167
They're not the best I've
ever had, I'm going to say.

00:06:47.167 --> 00:06:49.100
-Alright. But what is that?
Was that the salt and vinegar?

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:51.534
Or is that -- Doesn't matter.
-I think that was

00:06:51.534 --> 00:06:53.000
the cheese and bacon.
-Does it matter at this point?

00:06:53.000 --> 00:06:54.801
You're eating crickets.
-There's a wing on your desk.

00:06:54.801 --> 00:06:56.467
-Oh, my gosh.
-Sorry.

00:06:56.467 --> 00:06:58.267
-This is so --
-You can do it.

00:06:58.267 --> 00:06:59.734
-I know, but I'm not
in this movie.

00:06:59.734 --> 00:07:01.000
-Just chuck the whole thing.

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:03.934
-Yeah, right. Oh, my God.
Alright.

00:07:03.934 --> 00:07:06.133
-They're quite bitty.
They got stuck in there.

00:07:06.133 --> 00:07:07.634
-The leg just came off.

00:07:07.634 --> 00:07:09.234
[ Audience groans ]

00:07:11.033 --> 00:07:12.968
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:18.367 --> 00:07:24.534
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:07:24.534 --> 00:07:26.133
-It's good.

00:07:28.567 --> 00:07:30.834
How do you feel?

00:07:30.834 --> 00:07:33.200
-No, no, no. It's interesting.

00:07:33.200 --> 00:07:35.200
It's not that bad,
but I wouldn't choose --

00:07:35.200 --> 00:07:37.667
-It's the thought of it that's
worse than the actual thing.

00:07:37.667 --> 00:07:40.868
-It tastes like a veggie stick.

00:07:40.868 --> 00:07:43.133
-Yeah, well, you know,

00:07:43.133 --> 00:07:44.901
me and Nic Cage
were saying yesterday

00:07:44.901 --> 00:07:46.601
when we were doing press, we
were like, "Oh, you know what?"

00:07:46.601 --> 00:07:49.601
Like, I mean, if we got over
our fear of eating insects,

00:07:49.601 --> 00:07:51.334
really, it's like great protein,
low fat.

00:07:51.334 --> 00:07:53.801
It would solve world hunger
in many ways.

00:07:53.801 --> 00:07:56.267
So it's like we were thinking
that maybe we'd start up

00:07:56.267 --> 00:07:59.667
a brand of like
the Two Nicks Insect Emporium

00:07:59.667 --> 00:08:01.601
and start, like,
selling dried insects.

00:08:01.601 --> 00:08:02.834
So, you heard it here first.

00:08:02.834 --> 00:08:05.100
-Let's get working on that.
Yeah. Of course.

00:08:05.100 --> 00:08:06.167
I want to show everyone a clip.

00:08:06.167 --> 00:08:07.834
Here is Nicholas Hoult
in "Renfield."

00:08:07.834 --> 00:08:09.501
Take a look at this.

00:08:09.501 --> 00:08:12.100
-I work for Dracula.

00:08:12.100 --> 00:08:14.868
-What?
-Dracula.

00:08:14.868 --> 00:08:16.934
-Dracula.

00:08:16.934 --> 00:08:19.467
-Dracula.
-Count Dracula.

00:08:19.467 --> 00:08:24.267
-No. No. President Dracula.
Yes. Yes. Sorry. Count Dracula.

00:08:24.267 --> 00:08:27.067
I'm -- I'm his familiar. I --
You know, I tend to his needs,

00:08:27.067 --> 00:08:30.000
including care, feeding,
you know, anything,

00:08:30.000 --> 00:08:32.367
especially during
the daylight hours.

00:08:32.367 --> 00:08:35.834
-Like, you get
Dracula people to eat?

00:08:35.834 --> 00:08:37.534
-I do other stuff too.

00:08:37.534 --> 00:08:39.834
-Like what? Wash his cape?

00:08:39.834 --> 00:08:42.434
-No. That's dry clean only.

00:08:42.434 --> 00:08:43.634
-There you go.

00:08:43.634 --> 00:08:44.934
Nicholas Hoult.

00:08:44.934 --> 00:08:48.033
"Renfield" is in theaters
April 14th.

00:08:48.033 --> 00:08:50.534
We have magic from Penn and
Teller after the break.

00:08:50.534 --> 00:08:51.801
Stick around, everybody.

